#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151957 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "edfa7501-4de4-5087-80fe-af2a91e53487" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Larger Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "2acbb9f2-ae84-536f-86aa-a1ca9203362f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6517" :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}1036 & = & 555(1) & + & 481 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 555 & = & 481(1) & + & 74 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 481 & = & 74(6) & + & 37 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 74 & = & 37(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}37}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555$ is $37.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(1\\,036, 555) = 37.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9625025a-4737-54ac-b218-56909013019a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Two Three-Digit Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "012fbcd1-3203-5247-8294-32bf795aadaf" :question/math-academy-id "e-6858" :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}713 & = & 322(2) & + & 69 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 322 & = & 69(4) & + & 46 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 69 & = & 46(1) & + & 23 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 46 & = & 23(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}23}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322$ is $23.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(713, 322) = 23.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2cb28a0-b456-55cc-94c3-2a35edde21be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Integers are Coprime"}] :question/id #uuid "d6699fbf-a942-5828-ab49-8e961e47d3c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8767" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7$\n\n- $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 1$\n\n- $651$ and $574$ are coprime" :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}651 & = & 574\\cdot 1 & + & 77 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 574 & = & 77\\cdot 7 & + & 35 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 77 & = & 35\\cdot 2 & + & 7 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 35 & = & 7\\cdot 5 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{blue}7}.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $651$ and $574$ is $7\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) > 1,$ the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9625025a-4737-54ac-b218-56909013019a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Two Three-Digit Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37a34d4f-dba5-52ed-aee1-a93037faa536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409453} {:answer/id #uuid "5f971696-c2bb-5172-9655-1e8f79d3ca37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409458} {:answer/id #uuid "69a86ecf-fc9b-50ae-b2d4-ff14e18b2347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409459} {:answer/id #uuid "c022bea5-a059-5982-aa11-260c9a0a9901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409466} {:answer/id #uuid "efba8139-449a-55b3-b733-32891bc8cbfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c022bea5-a059-5982-aa11-260c9a0a9901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409466} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d257bcf-1305-53a0-b375-298047636220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c6a7c2f-ebd8-5018-a687-121a9ed1a14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132699" :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $490$ and $154.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}490 & = & 154(3) & + & 28 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 154 & = & 28(5) & + & 14 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 28 & = & 14(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $14.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $490$ and $154$ is $14.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(490,154)=14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9625025a-4737-54ac-b218-56909013019a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Two Three-Digit Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042cfcd9-4141-5040-af7a-11da39d2f295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409447} {:answer/id #uuid "16d39c9a-b022-5605-a137-5bd4b9d862dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409448} {:answer/id #uuid "32bfcd7d-9a8e-5097-bc46-02425b35eb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409452} {:answer/id #uuid "396b6544-d108-5e60-a16f-f4d6ee7d2dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409455} {:answer/id #uuid "e24dd563-bb32-5701-9d59-c54cefdf265b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e24dd563-bb32-5701-9d59-c54cefdf265b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409470} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f564e11-e3e9-51f3-8747-8c068bd5977d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b810733-c1e7-54dd-bed6-851e7f2c57d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-77116" :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $296$ and $185.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}296 & = & 185(1) & + & 111 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 185 & = & 111(1) & + & 74 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 111 & = & 74(1) & + & 37 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 74 & = & 37(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $37.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $296$ and $185$ is $37.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(296,185)=37.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "edfa7501-4de4-5087-80fe-af2a91e53487" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Larger Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e32bdd0-f9d1-58b1-8b93-45c8d5b59754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409451} {:answer/id #uuid "98240c83-8994-519e-a548-3061729c276a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409462} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2463c2-21b8-5cbc-9741-8d3671c7003d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409463} {:answer/id #uuid "d302a4d9-5639-55c4-90a4-a1230a646926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409469} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fc61c5-d17d-5208-a87b-4f64c685cbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f2463c2-21b8-5cbc-9741-8d3671c7003d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409463} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ac88175-39b1-5c55-8c89-8a3d7dc310fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9e7b480-a26a-5ec4-83ee-1874930791b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79627" :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $1\\,248$ and $816.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}1\\,248 & = & 816(1) & + & 432 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 816 & = & 432(1) & + & 384 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 432 & = & 384(1) & + & 48 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 384 & = & 48(8) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $48.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $1\\,248$ and $816$ is $48.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(1\\,248,816)=48.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "edfa7501-4de4-5087-80fe-af2a91e53487" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Larger Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01592b2f-8e81-5ca0-bfa0-bfc6f3b61a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409445} {:answer/id #uuid "024a7e0b-4683-51db-a9ad-cca4f4ab8a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409446} {:answer/id #uuid "38281564-4727-50bc-a7f5-8a2abe6f2fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409454} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9fde34-a565-5dcd-91cd-3bb595bab139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409461} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0590cc-8e43-5ead-91f7-a0a5429235c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a9fde34-a565-5dcd-91cd-3bb595bab139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409461} :answer-field/id #uuid "0904c841-acc8-5b0f-b722-e910e2527c45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee6022bc-35f0-5e0b-b95e-866627e7670f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79654" :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $2\\,522$ and $1\\,092.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}2\\,522 & = & 1\\,092(2) & + & 338 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 1\\,092 & = & 338(3) & + & 78 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 338 & = & 78(4) & + & 26 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 78 & = & 26(3) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $26.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $2\\,522$ and $1\\,092$ is $26.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(2\\,522,1\\,092)=26.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2cb28a0-b456-55cc-94c3-2a35edde21be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Integers are Coprime"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a76842-1f68-5a89-9f31-4d47c4cc64a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409449} {:answer/id #uuid "7670efc5-9bf9-5b1e-bd6d-fa7dd6519896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409460} {:answer/id #uuid "be5dedfb-bad9-5066-aef5-f2053df360b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409465} {:answer/id #uuid "c6752fc0-b3b1-57e7-8e8f-82c9abd8bb42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409467} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5a86ba-3ae6-5111-b287-2f20bc390233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea5a86ba-3ae6-5111-b287-2f20bc390233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409472} :answer-field/id #uuid "bab5c137-e686-5330-940a-11463985a910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e2b9768-6303-5aa7-8a02-db48065ae94a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98476" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1$\n- $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=3$\n- $5719$ and $2669$ are coprime" :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5719 & = & 2669\\cdot 2 & + & 381 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2669 & = & 381\\cdot 7 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 381 & = & 2\\cdot 190 & + & 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2 & = & 1\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $1.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $5719$ and $2669$ is $1:$\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1,$ the numbers are coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2cb28a0-b456-55cc-94c3-2a35edde21be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Integers are Coprime"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26eed0b8-bca8-534f-9777-f36e6dec7b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409450} {:answer/id #uuid "3d952b6c-e25b-5f69-9d2a-ff0c63a4d7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409456} {:answer/id #uuid "56cc2837-afdc-5789-99d5-e022a2cce2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409457} {:answer/id #uuid "b2848211-e932-5634-bc25-53a4c7b4a5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409464} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cbf23b-c664-5d3c-87b3-cd51f24d52ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d952b6c-e25b-5f69-9d2a-ff0c63a4d7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409456} :answer-field/id #uuid "79213bec-cbc2-5e7b-a011-da32a4e25d22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3936dcf8-3c18-5d25-b1ef-fee251049b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98480" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{gcd}(765,221)=17$\n- $\\text{gcd}(765,221)=1$\n- $765$ and $221$ are coprime" :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}765 & = & 221\\cdot 3 & + & 102 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 221 & = & 102\\cdot 2 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 102 & = & 17\\cdot 6 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $17.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $765$ and $221$ is $17:$\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(765,221)=17.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{gcd}(765,221)>1,$ the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
